  "line": "Syria, called Aram in the Old Testament, was the land north of Israel, home to Damascus and to kings who fought Israel and Judah.",
  "moments": [
    {
      "title": "Naaman the commander",
      "text": "Naaman, the commander of the king of Aram's army, was a great man, but he had leprosy. A young girl taken from Israel told his wife about the prophet in Samaria, and the king sent Naaman to Israel with a letter and gifts.",
      "passage": "2 Kings 5:1-6"
    },
    {
      "title": "Aram's king is enraged",
      "text": "The king of Aram was at war against Israel, but Elisha kept warning Israel's king. The angry king of Aram demanded to know which of his servants was on Israel's side.",
      "passage": "2 Kings 6:8-12"
    },
    {
      "title": "Elisha and Hazael in Damascus",
      "text": "Elisha came to Damascus while Ben-hadad, king of Aram, was sick. Hazael brought a gift and asked if the king would recover, and Elisha said the LORD had shown him that Hazael would be king over Aram.",
      "passage": "2 Kings 8:7-13"
    },
    {
      "title": "Aram marches on Jerusalem",
      "text": "Rezin, king of Aram, marched up to fight Jerusalem with Israel's king, but could not overpower the city. Aram's plot against Judah made Ahaz and his people tremble, and the LORD sent Isaiah to tell Ahaz not to fear.",
      "passage": "Isaiah 7:1-8"
    },
    {
      "title": "Jesus' fame spreads",
      "text": "News about Jesus spread all over Syria. People brought Him all who were ill, in pain, demon-possessed, having seizures, or paralyzed, and He healed them.",
      "passage": "Matthew 4:23-25"
    }
  ],
